#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2151f28-66ff-5cda-9211-5906f7882366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352340} {:answer/id #uuid "99f7b5e3-b4e0-5cd4-b4cd-b628df411ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352341} {:answer/id #uuid "6de58419-69b4-5be3-bcbc-a466ce4ffc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t(1-4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352342} {:answer/id #uuid "194cbdfc-f0e6-5b10-941a-073ef36a1618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352343} {:answer/id #uuid "d619cdef-c048-54c6-b481-b788d0467834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2151f28-66ff-5cda-9211-5906f7882366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352340} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4506bc4-5dbe-5b03-8639-77e63c997b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cc72486-d161-51c9-81d8-95cb93b64485" :question/math-academy-id "q-124656" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac7a92e-45db-5339-954a-3d12f0af502b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352347} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce5dc3a-e973-568d-84b3-65b310c6a3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352348} {:answer/id #uuid "5834f9df-e912-5850-8c34-b424f767e97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352349} {:answer/id #uuid "63102b81-f990-580b-9d83-074239c7f80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352350} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc63b1a-0f79-5065-ab5a-4326d53873c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc63b1a-0f79-5065-ab5a-4326d53873c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352351} :answer-field/id #uuid "63838486-62db-561a-b0a0-898d48bc4de6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0814c9bc-122f-53c2-a756-b81f1b80f4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-124661" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }((x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+6t]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+6\\pi )-(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3\\pi )] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}+6\\pi )-(\\frac{5}{4}+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce41c1b2-83ff-599a-bbd5-92d83d9143de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352354} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1cc6dd-9d67-5b90-8ac8-bc058e1c33c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352355} {:answer/id #uuid "62c715db-cb24-51e8-9823-4c18f652cc6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352356} {:answer/id #uuid "47ad0aab-fc84-5bb9-aaca-0945d6bf99b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352357} {:answer/id #uuid "3335272b-1466-5b2b-9c2e-eb80f3bc0c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3335272b-1466-5b2b-9c2e-eb80f3bc0c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352358} :answer-field/id #uuid "4251c9c6-fdf9-5223-88ef-f8a83ccbe5e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6bed643-ce3e-507d-aa72-c9eaa603d013" :question/math-academy-id "q-81098" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-x.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+(2t-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87f6b4ad-2f0f-5314-9435-64aadffba8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln 3 - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179202} {:answer/id #uuid "d52366e2-e0cc-54df-8a4a-7f6531517da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2 - \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179203} {:answer/id #uuid "de8b78d6-bc73-5cb4-871f-32180e6ecc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln 2 + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179204} {:answer/id #uuid "287c3b71-0bbf-5177-97b7-af5fc2c12bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 3 + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179205} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2fa12c-87bd-569a-8100-eeba0e559ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln 2 - \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8b78d6-bc73-5cb4-871f-32180e6ecc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln 2 + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179204} :answer-field/id #uuid "cac1d3e2-643d-53eb-9307-9f22f1f942a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2bdab86-46e0-5604-a07d-20d0ff29dcb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81178" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=(x+1)^{2}$ traversed from the point $P(0,1)$ to point $Q(1,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2(t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1+2(t+1)}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{t+1}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{1+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)]-[-\\frac{1}{0+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[-1+0] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4188b0c3-b5bb-55df-b23b-6eda1f545865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352361} {:answer/id #uuid "75779101-db5b-5d49-84a3-e4bf841bef31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352362} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8e7330-8fb7-53e3-a350-b9c4ee4205b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352363} {:answer/id #uuid "bb812ea7-a296-5786-a89b-3bba9cc888b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352364} {:answer/id #uuid "29722e4b-6cf6-5cf9-8b52-7451d0ade62d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75779101-db5b-5d49-84a3-e4bf841bef31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352362} :answer-field/id #uuid "19f207d4-f525-51a0-9baf-5fba3eda5400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3320ba2-2de7-59d7-ba39-d983351db61e" :question/math-academy-id "q-90848" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1+1)\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{4}-5{t}^{2}+2{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}(2)^{3}+\\frac{{2}^{6}}{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =32-\\frac{40}{3}+\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
